          "answer": "The narrative of Rabbi Zaid ibn Sanah in Unit 1 provides an extraordinary empirical proof of the Holy Prophet's (PBUH) divine mission. Rabbi Zaid, an esteemed Jewish theologian in Madina, had verified all biblical signs of the promised Messenger in Hazrat Muhammad (PBUH) except two: that his forbearance would always surpass his anger, and that harsh treatment would only heighten his benevolence.\n\nTo rigorously test these attributes, Rabbi Zaid approached the Prophet (PBUH) following a funeral prayer, violently seizing his cloak by the edges and insolently accusing the family of Abdul-Muttalib of debt delinquency days before the payment was legitimately due. When Hazrat Umar (RA) drew his sword in fury, the Prophet (PBUH) remained perfectly composed, providing a masterclass in conflict resolution by stating: 'You should have given us sincere counselling, rather than act as you did! Go and repay him his loan, and give him twenty Sa'a extra because you scared him.'\n\nWitnessing that the Prophet's generosity expanded in the face of provocation and that twenty extra Sa'a of dates were gifted to heal emotional distress, Rabbi Zaid immediately acknowledged that such transcendent morality could only belong to the authentic Messenger of Allah. He renounced his former faith on the spot and proclaimed the Shahadah.\n\nThis incident establishes that genuine leadership overcomes hostility through justice, kindness, and profound emotional intelligence."
