          "marks": "8 Marks",
          "outline": "I. Introduction to Both Masterpieces and Authors (Wordsworth vs. Dickinson)\nII. Conceptual Metaphors: Nature as Physical Revelation vs. Bird as Psychological Allegory\nIII. Imagery: Golden Lakeside Vistas vs. Stormy Gales and Chilly Seas\nIV. The Enduring Gift: Memory of Joy vs. Unconditional Emotional Resilience\nV. Conclusion: Two Immortal Visions of Inner Human Fortitude",
          "answer": "Both William Wordsworth's 'The Daffodils' and Emily Dickinson's 'Hope is the Thing with Feathers' explore how non-material forces sustain the human spirit through life's trials.\n\n1. Nature as Physical Entity vs. Nature as Mental Allegory:\n- In 'The Daffodils', Wordsworth interacts with an external, physical natural landscape (lake, trees, real golden flowers) whose memory later generates spiritual solace.\n- In 'Hope', Dickinson creates an internal, psychological allegory, conceptualizing hope as an unseen little bird perching within the soul, singing wordless tunes.\n\n2. Contrast of Imagery:\n- Wordsworth paints golden, sunlit, festive landscapes of spring, twinkling galaxies, and dancing waves.\n- Dickinson employs turbulent atmospheric imagery—fierce gales, howling storms, chill seas, and strangest lands—to prove the indomitable courage of hope.\n\n3. Common Spiritual Message:\nBoth poets affirm that true human wealth is immaterial. Wordsworth's 'inward eye' and Dickinson's 'little bird' both reside deep within the human psyche, offering unconditional comfort and joy without demanding any material price."
