          "content": "Summary of the Lesson:\n'The Two Bargains' illustrates the unparalleled philanthropy of Hazrat Usman Ghani (رضی اللہ عنہ). In the first bargain, when Medina faced severe water shortages, the Prophet (ﷺ) promised Paradise to whoever bought the sweet water well 'Bair-e-Roma'. Hazrat Usman bought half the well for 12,000 dirhams and later the entire well, dedicating it as a permanent public endowment where Muslims and Jews received free water. In the second bargain, during a catastrophic famine under Caliph Umar (رضی اللہ عنہ), a caravan of 1,000 camel-loads of food arrived for Hazrat Usman. Despite merchants and the Caliph offering up to nine times profit, Hazrat Usman refused them all, declaring he had sold his grain to Allah for a reward of ten to seven hundred times, and distributed the entire stock freely among the starving population."
