          "content": "Given Passage:\n\"The Jew learnt a bitter lesson. Whereas he had hoped to make more money, he made none at all. He went to Hazrat Usman and offered to sell the other half of the well also. Hazrat Usman could have refused to buy it, for the Muslims had no longer any difficulty, but such was his generosity that he paid the Jew whatever he asked for his share. The well was dedicated as an endowment to the people of Madina. The well exists in Medina to this day and is known as Baire Usman or Usman's well.\"\n\nSolved Questions:\nI. Main Idea of the Passage: The passage illustrates how the Jewish owner's greed was defeated by community solidarity, leading Hazrat Usman to purchase and perpetually endow the entire well of Bair-e-Roma for the public welfare.\nII. Three Adjectives in the Passage: 'bitter' (modifying lesson), 'more' (modifying money), 'other' (modifying half).\nIII. Tense of the Passage: Past Simple Tense (e.g., learnt, hoped, made, went, offered, paid, asked, was dedicated) combined with Past Modal Perfect ('could have refused').\nIV. Why the Jew went to Hazrat Usman: Because on Hazrat Usman's turn, all residents fetched two days' worth of water for free, leaving the Jew with zero customers on his commercial days and ruining his profits."
