      "longQuestions": [
        {
          "question": "1. Comprehensive Historical Evaluation: Analyze the Two Bargains of Hazrat Usman (رضی اللہ عنہ) as Milestones in Islamic Philanthropy.",
          "marks": "8 Marks",
          "outline": "I. Introduction: The Spiritual Concept of Trade with Allah (Tijarah Ma'a Allah)\nII. First Bargain: Socio-Economic Impact of Purchasing Bair-e-Roma\nIII. The Strategic Welfare Innovation of Public Endowment (Waqf)\nIV. Second Bargain: Famine Relief and Moral Leadership during Caliph Umar's Era\nV. Universal Lessons for Contemporary Wealth Distribution\nVI. Conclusion",
          "answer": "The life of Hazrat Usman ibn Affan (رضی اللہ عنہ) presents an extraordinary synthesis of commercial acumen and selfless philanthropy.\n\n1. The First Bargain (Bair-e-Roma):\nUpon the Hijrah, the monopoly of drinking water by a private merchant threatened the public health of the Muslim community. By investing 12,000 dirhams to acquire the well, Hazrat Usman established the first major public utility endowment (Waqf) in Islamic history. The equal provision of free water to Muslims and Jews alike established non-discriminatory public welfare.\n\n2. The Second Bargain (Famine Relief):\nDuring the severe famine (*Am al-Ramadah*) in Hazrat Umar's Caliphate, market hoarding and inflation threatened mass starvation. Hazrat Usman rejected lucrative commercial bids offering up to 900% profit from local merchants and the state. Instead, he invoked the divine economic principle: 'Whoever brings a good deed will have ten times the like thereof' (Al-An'am 6:160). He donated 1,000 camel-loads of flour to feed the entire city freely.\n\n3. Enduring Legacy: These two bargains demonstrate that Islamic economics prioritizes social survival and human dignity over monopolistic greed and market exploitation."
        },
        {
          "question": "2. Thematic & Ethical Analysis: Explore the Concept of Generosity in Islam Beyond Material Wealth.",
          "marks": "8 Marks",
          "outline": "I. The Islamic Definition of Generosity (Jood wa Sakhawah)\nII. Material Generosity (Zakat, Sadaqah, and Waqf)\nIII. Non-Material Generosity (Time, Knowledge, Emotional Support, Forgiveness)\nIV. The Psychological and Spiritual Benefits of Charity on the Giver and Receiver\nV. Conclusion",
          "answer": "In Islamic ethics, generosity transcends the simple act of giving money; it is a holistic mindset of benevolence toward all creation.\n\n1. Dimensions of Non-Material Generosity:\nAs emphasized in prophetic traditions, every act of goodness is charity (*Kullu Ma'rufin Sadaqah*). Generosity encompasses:\n• Donating Time & Energy: Assisting the elderly, volunteering during natural disasters, and mentoring youth.\n• Intellectual Charity: Sharing knowledge, teaching literacy, and providing sincere counsel without charge.\n• Emotional Generosity: Offering a welcoming smile, speaking gently, practicing patience, and forgiving those who insult us.\n\n2. The Model of the Rightly Guided Caliphs:\nHazrat Usman gave his immense commercial wealth; Hazrat Umar gave his relentless vigilance and physical energy; Hazrat Abu Bakr gave everything he possessed; Hazrat Ali gave profound wisdom and justice. Together, they demonstrated that generosity is the soul of human fraternity."
        },
        {
          "question": "3. Comprehensive Grammar Guide: Adverbs, Types, Positioning, Degrees of Comparison, and Adverbial Clauses.",
          "marks": "8 Marks",
          "outline": "I. Definition and Core Functions of Adverbs (Modifying Verbs, Adjectives, Adverbs)\nII. Classification of Adverbs (Manner, Time, Place, Frequency, Degree)\nIII. Syntactic Placement of Adverbs within Sentences\nIV. Comparative and Superlative Degrees of Adverbs (Regular, Irregular, -ly forms)\nV. Adverbial Phrases vs. Adverbial Clauses: Identification and Analysis",
          "answer": "Adverbs are indispensable grammatical elements that provide precise context regarding how, when, where, why, and to what extent actions occur.\n\n1. Classification of Adverbs:\n• Manner: *quietly, calmly, generously, attentively*\n• Time: *now, yesterday, soon, late*\n• Place: *here, there, outside, everywhere*\n• Frequency: *always, usually, often, rarely, never*\n• Degree: *very, almost, extremely, completely*\n\n2. Degrees of Comparison:\n• Regular (-ly adverbs): *beautifully -> more beautifully -> most beautifully*\n• Short adverbs: *late -> later -> latest*, *fast -> faster -> fastest*\n• Irregular adverbs: *well -> better -> best*, *badly -> worse -> worst*\n\n3. Adverbial Phrases:\nGroups of words acting as adverbs without a subject-verb core:\n• *in about an hour* (answers 'When?')\n• *better than my brother* (answers 'How?')\n\n4. Adverbial Clauses:\nDependent clauses beginning with subordinating conjunctions (*because, if, although, when, before, after*):\n• *Because no one was home* (Clause of Reason modifying 'robbed').\n• *If Basheer calls* (Clause of Condition modifying 'tell')."
        },
        {
          "question": "4. Character Study: The Leadership and Humanitarian Vision of Hazrat Usman Ghani (رضی اللہ عنہ).",
          "marks": "8 Marks",
          "outline": "I. Introduction: Hazrat Usman's Titles (Dhun-Noorayn, Al-Ghani)\nII. Moral Character: Modesty, Humility, and Calm Demeanor\nIII. Economic Vision: Wealth as a Sacred Trust from Allah\nIV. Legacy of Institutional Philanthropy (Waqf)\nV. Conclusion",
          "answer": "Hazrat Usman ibn Affan (رضی اللہ عنہ) holds a distinguished position in world history as a model leader who treated personal wealth as a divine trust.\n\n1. Titles and Noble Traits:\nNamed *Al-Ghani* (The Generous) and *Dhun-Noorayn* (Possessor of the Two Lights for marrying two daughters of the Prophet ﷺ), he was renowned for profound modesty (*Haya*) and calm wisdom. Despite being one of Arabia's richest merchants, his personal lifestyle remained austere.\n\n2. Ethical Business Philosophy:\nHazrat Usman never weaponized scarcity to exploit consumers. When water and food were scarce, instead of raising prices, he used his capital to eliminate monopolies, establish price stability, and distribute food unconditionally.\n\n3. Institutional Permanence: By transforming Bair-e-Roma into a self-sustaining Waqf, he created an economic model that has endured for 14 centuries, providing water and date harvests to the poor to this day."
        },
        {
          "question": "5. Analytical Essay on Economic Equity: Solutions to Wealth Disparity Inspired by the Medina Caliphate.",
          "marks": "8 Marks",
          "outline": "I. The Crisis of Modern Wealth Concentration and Famines\nII. The Dual Mechanism: Institutional State Welfare (Bait-ul-Mal) and Private Philanthropy (Waqf)\nIII. Prohibiting Exploitative Monopolies and Price Gouging\nIV. Applying Hazrat Usman's Model in Modern Pakistan\nV. Conclusion",
          "answer": "Modern global economics struggles with unprecedented wealth concentration, where the top 1% owns more than half the world's wealth while millions starve.\n\n1. The Prophetic and Caliphate Model:\nThe Second Bargain highlights the ideal partnership between state governance and private wealth:\n• Caliph Umar represented state responsibility, actively monitoring food supplies and spending public treasury (*Bait-ul-Mal*) funds.\n• Hazrat Usman represented private capital, rejecting speculative profits to ensure the survival of his fellow citizens.\n\n2. Practical Solutions for Today:\n• Eradicate Hoarding: Strictly prohibit artificial price hikes of basic foodstuffs, medicines, and water.\n• Revitalize Waqf Institutions: Channel corporate social responsibility into solar water filtration, public education, and free hospitals.\n• Progressive Redistribution: Implement transparent Zakat collection to eliminate extreme poverty.\n\n3. Conclusion: The Medina model proves that sustainable economic peace is achieved when wealth circulates for public good rather than accumulating in private vaults."
        }
      ]
