      "writingTasks": [
        {
          "task": "A. Paraphrase the First and Second Stanza of the Poem 'Hope is the Thing with Feathers'",
          "content": "Stanza 1 Paraphrase:\nHope is an invisible yet potent virtue compared to a gentle bird that makes its home in the innermost sanctuary of the human soul. Without uttering verbal words, it sings an unending, soothing melody of encouragement that never ceases, keeping human spirits alive through every phase of life.\n\nStanza 2 Paraphrase:\nThe comforting song of hope is experienced most sweetly during the storms and tribulations of life. It would require an exceptionally violent and cruel tempest to daunt, embarrass, or silence this tender bird which has provided warmth, peace, and reassurance to countless troubled hearts."
        },
        {
          "task": "B. Write a Comprehensive Summary of the Poem 'Hope is the Thing with Feathers'",
          "content": "In 'Hope is the Thing with Feathers', Emily Dickinson presents an inspiring, extended metaphorical portrayal of hope as a feathered songbird dwelling inside the human soul. The bird sings a wordless, universal melody that never stops, symbolizing that hope is an instinctive, everlasting spiritual presence within mankind.\n\nThe poet observes that the bird's song is heard sweetest in times of fierce gales and storms—meaning that hope's true worth and comforting power shine brightest when human beings endure pain, bereavement, and adversity. It would take an extraordinarily severe storm to crush or quiet this modest bird, which has comforted many weary souls throughout history.\n\nFinally, Dickinson reflects on her personal journey, noting that she has heard hope's uplifting song in the coldest, bleakest circumstances and on the strangest, most desolate seas of life. Yet, despite its relentless support, hope is completely selfless; it never asks for a single crumb or reward in return. Thus, hope remains humanity's greatest, most benevolent inner anchor."
        }
      ],
