        {
          "question": "LQ1. Discuss the multidimensional benefits of technology in healthcare, agriculture, and communication as outlined in Unit 10.",
          "answer": "Technology serves as the fundamental engine of modern human advancement across three vital domains:\n\n1. Healthcare & Life Sciences:\n- Diagnostic Precision: Advanced imaging and biochemical analyzers detect malignancies and chronic diseases at embryonic stages.\n- Treatment Quality: Computer-assisted surgery and automated monitoring elevate patient recovery rates.\n\n2. Agricultural Innovation & Biotechnology:\n- Climate-Resilient Crops: Genetic modification introduces drought- and pest-tolerant genes into staple crops.\n- Desert Reclamation: Modern precision irrigation and biotechnology enable food cultivation in arid terrains.\n\n3. Telecommunications & Global Networking:\n- Global Village: Instantaneous data transmission via internet and satellite networks eliminates geographical barriers.\n- Digital Commerce: E-commerce platforms empower consumers and entrepreneurs to transact globally within seconds.\n\nConclusion: Technological synergy across these sectors elevates human living standards, mitigates suffering, and drives sustained economic prosperity."
        },
        {
          "question": "LQ2. Detailed Exposition of Clauses: Main, Subordinate, Noun, Adjective, and Adverbial Clauses with illustrative examples.",
          "answer": "A clause is a syntactic unit containing a subject and a finite predicate verb.\n\n1. Main (Independent) Clause:\nCan stand alone as a complete thought. Example: 'He runs with great speed.'\n\n2. Subordinate (Dependent) Clause:\nCannot stand alone and depends on a main clause for full meaning.\n\nSubordinate clauses are classified into three types:\n- Noun Clause: Functions as subject, object, or complement.\n  Example: 'The angler said that he hadn't caught any fish.' ('that he hadn't caught...' is object of 'said').\n\n- Adjective (Relative) Clause: Modifies a noun or pronoun, introduced by relative pronouns (who, which, that, whose).\n  Example: 'The boys who misbehaved in the class were suspended.' ('who misbehaved' describes 'boys').\n\n- Adverb Clause: Modifies a verb, adjective, or adverb, indicating time, place, condition, concession, or cause.\n  Example: 'Though he didn't receive formal schooling, he is clever.' ('Though he didn't receive...' indicates concession).\n\nMastery of clause types empowers students to construct complex, sophisticated, and grammatically precise prose."
        },
        {
          "question": "LQ3. Write an analytical essay on 'Harnessing Renewable Energy (Hydro, Solar, Wind) for Sustainable Economic Development in Pakistan'.",
          "answer": "Energy is the lifeblood of industrial production, agriculture, and domestic livelihood. Relying on imported fossil fuels strains national foreign reserves and exacerbates environmental degradation. Transitioning to renewable energy is imperative for Pakistan's economic stability.\n\n1. Hydroelectric Power: Pakistan possesses immense river runoff potential. Mega-dams (such as Tarbela, Mangla, and Diamer-Bhasha) store seasonal monsoon floods, provide regulated irrigation water to the Indus basin, and generate cheap electricity.\n\n2. Photovoltaic Solar Energy: Blessed with over 300 sunny days annually, Pakistan can harness distributed rooftop solar and utility-scale solar parks to power tube wells, commercial centers, and households cost-effectively.\n\n3. Wind Corridors: The coastal wind corridors in Sindh (Jhimpir and Gharo) offer substantial wind energy generation capacity, supplying continuous clean power.\n\nConclusion: Investing in renewable technologies guarantees long-term energy security, lowers electricity tariffs, reduces carbon emissions, and ensures sustainable economic growth."
        },
        {
          "question": "LQ4. Compare and contrast traditional manual data recording with modern digital data management as discussed in the text.",
          "answer": "The transition from traditional paper-based archives to digital information systems represents a fundamental paradigm shift:\n\n1. Storage Volume & Bulkiness:\n- Traditional: Requires extensive physical archive rooms, voluminous paper files, and heavy shelving subject to clutter.\n- Digital: Terabytes of data are preserved virtually on compact disks, microchips, and cloud servers occupying zero physical space.\n\n2. Security & Confidentiality:\n- Traditional: Paper documents are vulnerable to fire, water damage, insect infestation, and unauthorized physical theft.\n- Digital: Encrypted passwords, biometric verification, and multi-factor authentication restrict access strictly to authorized proprietors.\n\n3. Retrieval Speed & Efficiency:\n- Traditional: Locating specific records manually through ledger indexes can take hours or days.\n- Digital: Search algorithms retrieve query results within milliseconds from anywhere in the world.\n\nConclusion: Modern digital data storage elevates organizational productivity, ensures data integrity, and supports sustainable, paperless administration."
        },
        {
          "question": "LQ5. Comprehensive Analysis of Adjective Formation, Degrees, and Adjective Ordering Rules from the Revision Exercises.",
          "answer": "Adjectives enrich descriptive language by qualifying nouns:\n\n1. Morphological Formation:\n- Suffixes like -ful (harm ➔ harmful, grace ➔ graceful), -al (music ➔ musical), -y (juice ➔ juicy, dust ➔ dusty), -ish (fool ➔ foolish, child ➔ childish), and -able (comfort ➔ comfortable).\n\n2. Degrees of Comparison:\n- Positive: Base quality (tall, wise, good, bad).\n- Comparative: Compares two entities (taller, wiser, better, worse, more harmful).\n- Superlative: Identifies the extreme among three or more (tallest, wisest, best, worst, most harmful).\n\n3. Royal Order of Adjectives:\nWhen multiple adjectives modify a single noun, English syntax follows the order:\n[Opinion] ➔ [Size] ➔ [Age] ➔ [Shape] ➔ [Colour] ➔ [Origin] ➔ [Material] ➔ [Purpose] + Noun.\nExample: 'He bought a handsome (opinion) new (age) English (origin) car.'\n\nApplying these principles ensures stylistic harmony and grammatical precision in written compositions."
        }
