          "content": "In 'The Old Woman', Joseph Campbell pays a tender, lyrical tribute to the beauty, dignity, and serenity of old age. The poet asserts that true beauty does not vanish with youth; rather, it transforms into a higher spiritual grace.\n\nIn the first stanza, Campbell compares an elderly woman's wrinkled face to a pure white candle glowing in a holy shrine, symbolizing purity, sanctity, and reverence. In the second stanza, her state is likened to the spent radiance of the winter sun. Having completed her life's grueling toil ('travail done') and raised her family, her presence radiates soothing, mild warmth. In the final stanza, her children have grown up and departed ('her brood gone from her'). Free from domestic turmoil, her mind rests in deep contemplation, as serene and unruffled as the still pool of water beneath a ruined watermill.\n\nThe poem beautifully captures the peace, fulfillment, and divine tranquility that crown a life of selfless devotion."
