      "grammar": [
        {
          "topic": "Transitional Devices & Connectors of Cause, Effect, and Contrast",
          "explanation": "Transitional words link paragraphs and sentences cohesively:\n- Cause & Effect: therefore, consequently, as a result, hence ('The pipes leaked; *consequently*, the water was contaminated').\n- Contrast: however, nevertheless, on the other hand, whereas ('Complaints were filed; *however*, no action was taken').\n- Addition: furthermore, moreover, in addition ('*Moreover*, the power outages damaged laboratory appliances').",
          "examples": [
            "Furthermore, open drains breed dangerous mosquitoes.",
            "Consequently, schools were forced to close early.",
            "However, the authorities remained indifferent."
          ]
        },
        {
          "topic": "Subject-Verb Inversion after Negative Adverbials",
          "explanation": "When sentences begin with negative or restrictive adverbs (rarely, seldom, nowhere, not only), the auxiliary verb precedes the subject ('Rarely *have we witnessed* such administrative negligence').",
          "examples": [
            "Not only did they ignore complaints, but they also delayed repairs.",
            "Seldom has the community faced such acute load-shedding."
          ]
        }
      ],
      "writing": [
        {
          "title": "Complete Examination Letter: Letter to the Editor regarding Rampant Unscheduled Load-Shedding",
          "content": "Examination Hall,\nPeshawar.\nNovember 12, 2026.\n\nThe Editor,\nThe Daily Express Tribune,\nPeshawar.\n\nSubject: Crisis of Prolonged Unscheduled Load-Shedding in Kohat Road Area.\n\nSir,\nThrough the courtesy of your esteemed newspaper, I wish to invite the immediate attention of PESCO (Peshawar Electric Supply Company) and the Ministry of Energy toward the intolerable power outages in our locality.\n\nFor the past month, residents have been subjected to 12 to 14 hours of unscheduled load-shedding daily. This unannounced black-out occurs during critical morning hours and late evenings, severely disrupting student board exam preparations, shutting down small commercial workshops, and disabling water supply tube-wells. Patients relying on medical oxygen machines and infants are enduring acute distress in the suffocating heat.\n\nRepeated visits to the local PESCO complaints center have yielded only false promises. I urge the higher authorities to enforce an equitable load-management timetable, repair malfunctioning grid transformers, and ensure uninterrupted power supply during examination hours.\n\nYours sincerely,\nMohammad Bilal\n(President, Student Welfare Association)"
        }
      ]
