          "marks": "8 Marks",
          "outline": "I. Introduction: Leigh Hunt and the Sufi Lore of Ibrahim Ibn Adham\nII. The Conflict Between Ritual Piety and Altruistic Humanism\nIII. Poetic Structure: Masterful Use of Heroic Couplets and Archaic Register\nIV. Layered Symbolism: Moonlight, Lily, Book of Gold, and Wakening Light\nV. The Climax: Divine Validation of Humanitarian Love",
          "answer": "Leigh Hunt's 'Abou Ben Adhem' is one of the most sublime and universally revered lyric poems in the English language.\n\n1. Narrative Arc and Theological Insight:\nThe poem dramatizes an encounter between Abou Ben Adhem and an angel. When Abou discovers his name is missing from the list of those who love God, he does not despair. Instead, his humble plea to be recorded as 'one that loves his fellow men' leads to the triumphant climax on the second night, where his name leads all the rest.\n\n2. The Triumph of Humanism (Khidmat-e-Khalq):\nHunt demonstrates that God needs no human favors, but His vulnerable creation does. Therefore, the only tangible proof of divine love is selfless service to humanity.\n\n3. Metrics and Poetic Craftsmanship:\nComposed in eighteen lines of crisp heroic couplets (AABBCCDDEEFFGGHHII) in iambic pentameter, the poem uses archaic verbs ('writest', 'blest') and pronouns ('thou', 'thee') to evoke solemn biblical and Qur'anic gravity.\n\nConclusion:\nThe poem stands as an eternal beacon reminding mankind that love of God and love of humanity are one and the same."
